  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>We know <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mi>I</mi></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>I</mi></math> curve for a ohmic devices is a straight line passing through origin. Due to some errors/carelessness during experiments, all points may not come on the same line. In this situation, we draw the most appropriate curve.</p><p>From given&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>I</mi></math>&nbsp;curve,<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2.5</mn><mn>2.5</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math></p>"
  }
